## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical knowledge of multi-tenant isolation for analytics stacks on GCP, with particular focus on data access controls, encryption mechanisms, and network security under production load conditions.

## Key Concepts

- BigQuery Row-Level Security (RLS) and column-level access policies for fine-grained data isolation
- Dataflow worker pools with dedicated service account per tenant to enforce least privilege
- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) per tenant with envelope encryption for additional protection
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls to establish network boundaries and prevent data exfiltration
- Per-tenant observability with dedicated Cloud Logging sinks and alerting pipelines
- CI validation using synthetic tenants to continuously verify isolation guarantees

## Code Example

```javascript
// Terraform-like JS object to illustrate per-tenant config
const tenantConfig = {
  tenantId: "tenant-001",
  bigQuery: {
    datasetId: "tenant_001_analytics",
    rowLevelSecurity: true,
    columnPolicies: ["restricted_fields"]
  },
  dataflow: {
    workerPool: "tenant-001-pool",
    serviceAccount: "sa-tenant-001@project.iam.gserviceaccount.com",
    scopes: ["bigquery.readonly", "storage.readonly"]
  },
  encryption: {
    cmekKeyRing: "tenant-001-keyring",
    cmekKeyName: "tenant-001-cmek",
    envelopeEncryption: true
  },
  network: {
    privateServiceConnect: true,
    vpcServiceControls: true,
    ingressPolicies: ["allow-analytics-ui"],
    egressPolicies: ["deny-all-external"]
  },
  logging: {
    logSink: "tenant-001-logs",
    metricFilter: "tenant=tenant-001"
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design principled, automated cross-project containment in GCP at scale, with precise identity boundaries, network segmentation, credential hygiene, and auditable rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-project IAM and Workload Identity Federation
- Private Service Connect, Shared VPC, and VPC Service Controls
- Secret Manager + IAM conditions for short-lived credentials
- Synthetic tenants for end-to-end containment validation
- Immutable Audit Logs and rollback verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: rotate a tenant secret and revoke tokens on anomaly
async function containmentStep(tenantId){
  await revokeTokens(tenantId);
  await rotateSecret(tenantId);
  await quarantineNetwork(tenantId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the containment flow under simulated blast-radius events?
- What metrics indicate containment success vs. false positives across projects?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to secure public Cloud Run endpoints for multiple partners using standard GCP controls.

## Key Concepts
- Least-privilege IAM per partner
- IAP for authenticating users to Cloud Run
- Per-partner isolation via separate service accounts or gateway policies
- Credential rotation and auditability
- Synthetic tests to validate misconfig denial

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test: simulate non-permitted access and expect HTTP 403
const token = obtainTokenFor('partnerX');
fetch('https://api.example.run.app/endpoint', { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }})
  .then(response => {
    if (response.status === 403) {
      console.log('✓ Access properly blocked for unauthorized partner');
    } else {
      console.error('✗ Security misconfiguration detected');
    }
  });
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to apply concrete Google Cloud controls for multi-tenant isolation at runtime, beyond theoretical concepts, with a practical path from IAM to data and storage gates.

## Key Concepts

- IAM Conditions for per-tenant API access
- Storage prefixes and signed URLs for per-tenant data
- Per-tenant logging/alerting for exfiltration attempts
- CI gates that detect misconfig and roll back changes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple tenant check in API middleware
function authorize(req, res, next) {
  const tenantFromToken = req.user?.tenantId;
  const requestedTenant = req.params.tenantId;
  if (tenantFromToken !== requestedTenant) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the CI gate if a misconfig slips through?
- What are trade-offs of per-tenant prefixes vs separate buckets for storage isolation?
